  "conclusion": "One reproduced cache-integrity gap should be closed before the remaining ports are dispatched. No confirmed native/PEFT arithmetic or projection-path defect was found. Both completed Qwen GPU receipts report exact parity, matched reviewed source, and discard the positive-accuracy claim. Inkling still requires actual eight-GPU qualification.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "CAT-001",
      "priority": "P2",
      "status": "reproduced",
      "title": "Verify the bytes of cached configuration and base snapshots before using pinned identities",
      "locations": [
        {
          "file": "experiments/catalog/factory.py",
          "start_line": 21,
          "end_line": 34,
          "role": "local_asset only checks existence and byte count"
        },
        {
          "file": "experiments/catalog/factory.py",
          "start_line": 37,
          "end_line": 50,
          "role": "load_portal checks model.safetensors but does not bind config.json to its recorded digest"
        },
        {
          "file": "experiments/catalog/factory.py",
          "start_line": 53,
          "end_line": 74,
          "role": "load_base and the explicit base_path override accept size-matching snapshots"
        },
        {
          "file": "experiments/catalog/common.py",
          "start_line": 38,
          "end_line": 47,
          "role": "reuse cache selection admits equal-length changed files"
        }
      ],
      "trigger": "A file in an accepted canonical/reuse cache or base_path override differs from the pinned snapshot while retaining its byte count. The current process does not consume a checksum receipt for that concrete path.",
      "effect": "The runner can report the manifest revision while loading changed model/tokenizer/configuration bytes. For an adapter, changing alpha alters its applied scaling even when the safetensors checksum is correct. Native roundtrip and native/PEFT parity can still agree on the same changed adapter, so those gates do not establish published identity.",
      "evidence": {
        "artifact": "portal-qwen3-4b",
        "original_alpha": 16,
        "loaded_alpha": 32,
        "loaded_scaling": 4,
        "config_same_byte_length": true,
        "expected_raw_config_sha256": "3848aec36900aa1f890aaa629e3b67b2cdf2b2e48e81ea423971e32cf15420f5",
        "changed_config_sha256": "70718d88dbe57cd4627c050d4f2120ebd6f6be5f1d5b3fc8969400e93db072a2",
        "config_substitutions": 1,
        "load_accepted": true,
        "disk_unchanged": true,
        "scope": "In-memory json.load substitution models a same-length config corruption. No cache or catalog file was changed."
      },
      "reproduction": "Read the real portal-qwen3-4b config, replace the 16 in its alpha field with 32 without changing serialized length, and substitute that parsed JSON only for the corresponding json.load call using unittest.mock.patch. Call the real factory.load_portal against the published, hash-verified safetensors. It returns alpha=32 and scaling=4.0. Re-read the file and assert its bytes are unchanged. This is a non-mutating counterexample, not evidence that the real cache was corrupt.",
      "affected_models": "All seven catalog entries share these admission paths; base_path also covers the proposed Inkling node-local checkpoint.",
      "existing_mitigation": "download.py checks every downloaded file against SHA256 or the Git blob SHA1. Existing successful download receipts reduce the current-run risk. That integrity check is not enforced by load_base/load_portal and is not tied to a later concrete cache path.",
      "requested_change": "Reuse a single content-verification function at the load boundary, or require a verified immutable snapshot receipt tied to the exact selected path. Check the adapter config.json digest (entry.raw_config_sha256 is already present) and all loaded base/tokenizer metadata and shard identities, including the override path. Add a focused same-length config-corruption rejection check.",
      "claim_boundary": "The two completed Qwen receipts remain observed GPU parity results. This finding alone does not show their cached bytes were wrong."
    }
  ],
